\title{sheepdog: sample, validate and calibrate BirdNET detections}
\description{
sheepdog draws clips of BirdNET predictions for manual review,
summarises the verdicts, calibrates the confidence scores into
probabilities of true detection and builds detection histories and
community matrices from them. Start with \code{vignette("sheepdog")}.
}
\section{Glossary}{

The terms below are used with these meanings throughout the
documentation and the vignettes.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Prediction} or \strong{detection}: one row of a BirdNET selection
table, a species code with a confidence score for a time window of a
recording.
\item \strong{Confidence} or \strong{score}: the BirdNET output in (0, 1). It is not
a probability that the prediction is correct until calibrated.
\item \strong{Verdict}: the outcome of the manual review of a clip, \code{"positive"}
(true positive, TP) or \code{"negative"} (false positive, FP).
\item \strong{Precision}: the share of the reviewed predictions above a score
threshold that are true positives, TP / (TP + FP). Weighted precision
weights each clip by \code{1 / inclusion_prob}.
\item \strong{Occasion}: the sampling unit of the random designs and of the
occupancy export, one recording (\code{occasion_unit = "file"}) or one
minute of a recording (\code{"minute"}); see
\code{vignette("crywolf-integration")}.
\item \strong{Inclusion probability} (\code{inclusion_prob}): the probability that a
clip or occasion was drawn, \code{n_drawn / n_candidates} within its
stratum and score band. Weights \code{1 / inclusion_prob} make the drawn
units stand for all the candidates.
\item \strong{Recall ceiling}: the share of the occasions in which a species
called (by manual annotation of whole recordings) that have at least
one prediction of it above \code{min_confidence}; no threshold recovers
more (see \code{\link[=recall_ceiling]{recall_ceiling()}}).
\item \strong{P(TP)}: the probability that a prediction is a true positive,
predicted by a calibration model from its score, species and site.
\item \strong{Calibration model}: a logistic regression of the verdicts on the
logit of the score, \code{logit_conf}, with species and site effects (see
\code{\link[=calibration_models]{calibration_models()}} and \code{\link[=fit_calibration]{fit_calibration()}}).
\item \strong{Calibration intercept and slope}: the coefficients of a logistic
regression of held-out verdicts on the logit of the predicted P(TP).
A well calibrated model has intercept 0 and slope 1; a slope below 1
means probabilities that are too extreme.
\item \strong{\code{p_min}}: the P(TP) above which a detection is taken as reliable
when detection histories are built.
\item \strong{Detection history}: for each site, species and night, whether the
species was detected by at least \code{min_detections_night} reliable
detections (see \code{\link[=detection_history]{detection_history()}}).
\item \strong{R-hat, bulk and tail ESS}: convergence diagnostics of a Bayesian
fit, the potential scale reduction factor and the effective sample
sizes of the centre and of the tails of the posterior (see
\code{\link[=calibration_diagnostics]{calibration_diagnostics()}}).
}
}

\section{Option arguments}{

Arguments that take one of a set of strings (such as \code{engine},
\code{sampling_mode} or \code{occasion_unit}) are case-sensitive. Most are
matched with \code{match.arg()}, which also accepts unambiguous partial
matches: \code{engine = "brms"} and \code{engine = "b"} work, but \code{engine = "BRMS"} raises an error listing the allowed values. The others are
compared exactly, without partial matching.
}

\section{Input tables and missing values}{

Every function that takes a table accepts a \code{data.frame}, a tibble or a
\code{data.table} and converts it to a tibble on entry, so the result does not
depend on the class of the input. Numeric columns lose extra classes
(such as those of the 'units' package) and list columns among the
columns a function uses raise an error.

Rows with a missing value in a column a function needs (the score and
the audio path of a prediction; the variables of a calibration formula;
the score of a clip reviewed with \code{"top"}, \code{"random_detections"} or
\code{"score_bands"}) are left out with a message that says how many. Set
\code{options(sheepdog.missing = "error")} to stop with an error instead.
Missing values are never imputed. A reviewed clip without a score is
not a missing value when it comes from \code{"random_recordings"} (whole
recordings have no score); when a table of reviewed clips has no
\code{sampling_mode} column, every clip without a score is taken as such a
recording and left out of the precision and the thresholds without a
message.
}

